rom point 9: All red, green are furry. Bob is green, so he is furry. That's consistent.\n\nFrom point 11: If Bob is furry and red, then he is round. But we don't know if Bob is red yet. Wait, but according to point 6, since Bob is round and cold, he must be red. So that would make Bob red.\n\nBut let's check if there's any contradiction. Let's see:\n\n- Bob is green (point 1)\n- All green are blue (point 5), so Bob is blue\n- Bob is round (point 1)\n- Bob is cold (point 1)\n- Therefore, according to point 6, Bob is red.\n\nBut wait, let's check if Bob being red affects anything else. From point 12: Red things are big. Bob is big, so that's consistent.\n\nFrom point 13: All blue are furry, which Bob is, so that's consistent.\n\nFrom point 9: All red, green are furry, which Bob is, so that's consistent.\n\nFrom point 11: If Bob is furry and red, then he is round. But since Bob is both furry and red, this would imply he is round, which he is. So that's consistent.\n\nBut wait, let's think again. If Bob is red, then from point 12, red things are big, which Bob is. Also, from point 1, Bob is big. So that's consistent.\n\nBut let's check if Bob being red is possible without contradiction. Let's see:\n\n- Bob is green (point 1)\n- All green are blue (point 5), so Bob is blue\n- Bob is round (point 1)\n- Bob is cold (point 1)\n- Therefore, according to point 6, Bob is red.\n\nSo that seems to be the conclusion. Therefore, the statement \"Bob is red\" is true.\n</think>\n\nThe statement \"Bob is red\" is true based on the given information.\n\n**Answer: A) True**",
