ent \"The squirrel is cold\" is false. So the answer would be B) False.\n\nBut wait, let's make sure there's no other information that affects this. For example, point 10 says the squirrel is nice, but that doesn't directly affect whether it's cold or not. Point 11 says if something eats the squirrel, then the squirrel eats the mouse. But again, that's about the squirrel's eating behavior, not its coldness.\n\nPoint 12: If something visits the bear and it is green, then it is nice. But we don't know if the bear is green or not. However, point 16 says if the bear is not green, then the bear likes the lion. But that doesn't directly affect the squirrel's coldness.\n\nPoint 17: If something visits the bear, then the bear likes the lion. Again, not directly related to the squirrel's coldness.\n\nPoint 18: If something likes the lion and it is blue, then the lion is nice. But again, not directly relevant.\n\nPoint 19: If something eats the mouse, then it likes the lion. But again, not directly relevant.\n\nPoint 20: If the bear is not green, then the bear likes the lion. But again, not directly relevant.\n\nPoint 21: If something visits the bear, then the bear likes the lion. Again, not directly relevant.\n\nSo the only direct information is that the squirrel is not cold (point 9). Therefore, the statement \"The squirrel is cold\" is false. So the answer is B) False.\n</think>\n\nThe statement \"The squirrel is cold\" is false because the context indicates that the squirrel is not cold.\n\n**Answer: B) False**",
